{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man has a discharge.</b> I might think a זָב is impure from any place — therefore Scripture says מִבְּשָׂרוֹ, 'from his flesh' — and not all of his flesh. Now that Scripture has distinguished between flesh and flesh, I have grounds to reason a fortiori: he is impure in the case of זָב and she is impure in the case of זָבָה — just as the זָבָה is impure with lesser impurity — 'נִדָּה' — and impure with severe impurity — 'זִיבָה' — so too the זָב is impure with lesser impurity — 'קֶרִי' — and impure with severe impurity — 'זִיבָה' (ibid.)." "<b>His discharge is impure.</b> This teaches about the drop, that it imparts impurity. זִיבָה resembles the water of barley dough, and it is pressed out; and it resembles the white of a beaten egg — שִׁכְבַת זֶרַע is viscous, like the white of an egg that is not beaten."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:33.215495135Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}